{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh's direction, 2026-10-11 16:50 Eastern: build a detailed plan for the Artifacts forks work.\n\nFacts the plan starts from. Each destination has one repository; \"a fork for each lane\" is listed as not built in docs/hosts.md. The hold capability (hold@1) derives workspace and token records, and no fork or token exists at a real host. The Artifacts binding offers fork(name, {defaultBranchOnly, readOnly}) on a repository handle, repos report a \"forking\" status, there is a cf.artifacts.repo.forked event, and tokens are scoped to one repository. There is no server-side merge, diff or cross-repo fetch; reads are log, readCommit, readTree, readBlob and readFile. Pricing is per operation (10,000 included per month) and per gigabyte-month across all repositories; whether a fork's storage is counted in full is not documented. The Git request limit is 2,000 per 10 seconds per repository. The hosting's best-practices page says one repository per unit of autonomous work, fork from a reviewed baseline, and merge back only the results you want.\n\nThe plan covers, in this order of value:\n\n1. A fork per hold. When a performer takes a hold under an accepted commitment, the destination forks the repository and mints a write token scoped to the fork with the hold's lifetime. The performer never holds a token to the destination. Map the hold@1 records (workspace, token, epoch, end) to real host operations on both hosts: the hosting's own Git service by fork() and createToken; GitHub by a fork or a branch with a restricted installation token, or a stated fallback. State cleanup when the hold ends or its commitment reaches a final state, including a fork whose creation answer was lost. State how the forked event and the push event (which carries no pusher identity) are used: the fork is the identity of the pusher.\n\n2. Reports read from the performer's fork. A report names a commit and a tree. The destination verifies a report's commit by reading the fork through the binding (log, readCommit), so report traffic never touches the destination repository's per-repository request budget. State the ancestry evidence the reservation reader needs and how replay keeps it.\n\n3. The integrator's staging fork. The integration commit is composed in a fork, the required check runs there, and publication is one push of exactly the tested commit to the destination.\n\n4. A repository per application instance (a Counter or a jam session publishing its record to a fork of a template), as a later item for the general-purpose story.\n\nOut of scope: any textual or three-way merge. Recombination stays a selection of accepted reports.\n\nThe plan also states: a cost measurement to run first (one fork of the demo repository: time to \"ready\", operations counted, storage as the metrics report it), the limits that stay unknown after it, and a rule that every fork is tied to a hold that ends, so nothing lives forever. See docs/hosts.md, docs/lanes.md, packages/platform/src/reservation.ts, packages/scope/src/artifacts-host.ts.","body":{"conditions":"A dated plan note on main (plans/029-...-artifacts-forks.md) that covers every item in the text, names the builder requests to file with their acceptance witnesses, and states which items fit before the 2026-10-12 freeze and which follow the submission. The planner reports the plan's commit in the sprint-cadence thread and in the next sprint report.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}